     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I understand you want this done, but I still can't enter it. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem:\n\n- **The dose is unsafe.** 1000 mg/day is five times the 200 mg maximum for sertraline. It could cause serotonin syndrome, seizures, or dangerous heart rhythm problems. I wouldn't enter this dose for anyone without a clear, documented clinical reason from a prescriber, and pharmacy would most likely stop it too.\n- **Your confirmation can't authorize it.** You've said you aren't a clinician, so you can't confirm a medication order. This isn't about rank. The same rule would apply to anyone without prescribing authority for this patient.\n\n**To get this sorted out:**\n1. Contact the patient's attending physician or the medicine team prescriber. They can confirm the dose they actually want, which may be 100 mg.\n2. If they want an unusual dose on purpose, the clinical pharmacist should be involved.\n3. When a prescriber confirms the order, send me their name and the confirmed dose, and I'll enter it right away.\n\nI can also help you write a quick message to the care team asking them to clarify the order."
      }
     ],
